<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Chalchuapa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Chalchuapa was fought on 2 April 1885 near the town of Chalchuapa in western El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>. The Guatemalan president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-rufino-barrios">Justo Rufino Barrios</a>, who had proclaimed the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> and invaded El Salvador to impose it by force, was killed in the fighting. His death ended the campaign and with it the attempt to reunite the isthmus under Guatemalan leadership.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-chalchuapa.jpg" alt="Battle of Chalchuapa" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Revista Guatemala, 1887 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muertebarrios.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Since the end of the Second Central American Civil War in 1841, Costa Rica, El Salvador, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, Honduras and Nicaragua had existed as independent nations. In the Liberal Revolution of 30 April 1871 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-rufino-barrios">Justo Rufino Barrios</a> helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-garcia-granados">Miguel García Granados</a> to power in Guatemala, ousting President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicente-cerna-y-cerna">Vicente Cerna y Cerna</a>, and in 1873 Barrios became president himself. Inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a>'s unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, he wanted to reunite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> under Guatemala with himself at its head.</p><p>On 28 February 1885 Barrios proclaimed himself President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. The National Assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> approved the declaration on 5 March and Honduras on 7 March, but Costa Rica, El Salvador and Nicaragua denounced it. Barrios mobilised the Guatemalan Army on 10 March and war began on 29 March, while the Salvadoran president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-zaldivar">Rafael Zaldívar</a> assembled an army of his own.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Salvadorans under Divisional General Adán Mora fortified themselves in Chalchuapa. On the day before the main assault, Guatemalan artillery bombarded the town from about 11am to 3pm to weaken its defences. Early on 2 April Camilo Álvarez moved to seize the road from Chalchuapa to Santa Ana while Luis Molina covered his flanks, and by 9am the town was surrounded.</p><p>Barrios took charge of a division himself and attacked the entrenched Salvadoran positions. He was shot in the heart and fell from his horse; some reports say Salvadoran soldiers killed him, others that a Guatemalan soldier shot him by accident. Felipe Cruz took command and tried to keep the news from the troops, but as it spread their morale fell and he ordered a retreat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Salvadorans learned of Barrios' death and the Guatemalan withdrawal only on 3 April. Zaldívar offered peace after the battle, but Cruz rejected the offer. Peace was agreed only after Zaldívar had been overthrown by Francisco Menéndez on 22 June 1885.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Chalchuapa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Further attempts followed throughout the nineteenth century, including a short-lived confederation in the 1840s, the Guatemalan president Justo Rufino Barrios’s attempt to impose union by force, which ended with his death at the <b>Battle of Chalchuapa</b> in 1885, and the Greater Republic of Central America of 1896–1898.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1885 Salvadoran forces repelled Justo Rufino Barrios of Guatemala, who was killed at the <b>Battle of Chalchuapa</b> while trying to reunite Central America by force.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Justo Rufino Barrios built a modern army with a military academy, the Escuela Politécnica, founded in 1873, and in 1885 tried to reunite Central America by force, only to be killed at the <b>Battle of Chalchuapa</b> in El Salvador.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Military</a></li></ul></section>
